Certainly! Here's a complex JavaScript program using advanced features such as async/await, Promises, and destructuring assignment:

```javascript
const fetchData = async (url) => {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
  return response.json();
};

const processData = async () => {
  try {
    const [users, posts] = await Promise.all([
      fetchData('https: 
      fetchData('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts')
    ]);

    const userPosts = users.map(user => {
      const { id, name } = user;
      const userSpecificPosts = posts.filter(post => post.userId === id);
      return {
        name,
        posts: userSpecificPosts.map(({ title, body }) => ({ title, body }))
      };
    });

    console.log(userPosts);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
  }
};

processData();
```

This code uses asynchronous functions and handles multiple Promises with `Promise.all`, as well as using destructuring to access object properties efficiently.